-- title:
--   Riemann–Hurwitz for an unramified Kummer cover of prime degree
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field and $F$ a field equipped with an $L$-algebra structure such that some $x \in F$ is transcendental over $L$ and $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $L(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $q$ be a prime whose image in $L$ is nonzero, and let $f \in F$ be an element that is not a $q$-th power, i.e. $g^q \neq f$ for every $g \in F$, and whose order is divisible by $q$ at every place: for each $P$ in `Place L F` — a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $L$, distinct from all of $F$, and a principal ideal ring — one has $q \mid \operatorname{ord}_P f$, where $\operatorname{ord}_P f$ is minus the logarithm of the value of $f$ under the adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of $P$. Let $F'$ be a field that is an $L$-algebra and an $F$-algebra compatibly (an $L$-$F$-$F'$ scalar tower) and is a splitting field of $X^q - f$ over $F$. Then, as integers, $$\operatorname{genusFF}(L, F') - 1 = q\,\bigl(\operatorname{genusFF}(L, F) - 1\bigr),$$ where $\operatorname{genusFF}(L, \cdot)$ denotes the $L$-dimension of $H^1$ of the zero divisor.
--
--   This is the Riemann–Hurwitz genus formula in the special case of the degree-$q$ Kummer extension $F(f^{1/q})/F$ that is unramified at every place, the hypothesis $q \mid \operatorname{ord}_P f$ for all $P$ forcing all ramification indices to be $1$; the case $q = \operatorname{char} L$ is excluded by the assumption that $q$ is invertible in $L$. It is used in the analysis of regular prolongations, via [`AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_pow_eq_of_residue_eq_pow_of_finrank_eq_of_isAlgClosed`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_pow_eq_of_residue_eq_pow_of_finrank_eq_of_isAlgClosed).
